We'll begin with relative references. What I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:40.720 --> 02:21:45.840
here is a list of the numbers one through 10.&nbsp;
And I want to take each of those numbers and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:45.840 --> 02:21:51.200
want to multiply it times two. And I'm going&nbsp;
to start by writing a formula, all I do is I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:51.200 --> 02:21:55.679
type equals, that tells Google Sheets, I'm going&nbsp;
to write a formula. And then I click on the cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:55.680 --> 02:22:04.241
I want to multiply it's one over to the left, and&nbsp;
I multiply it times two, I do asterisk to great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:04.240 --> 02:22:09.359
and when I do that, you can see a preview of&nbsp;
what these answers gonna be. I just hit it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:09.359 --> 02:22:15.040
there's a two, if you want to see again, what's&nbsp;
going on here, just double click on this cell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:16.479 --> 02:22:20.000
And it will highlight the cell is referred&nbsp;
to and you see the color here is orange and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:20.000 --> 02:22:23.920
the matches here. If you have more than one cell&nbsp;
reference, it will do them in different colors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:24.640 --> 02:22:29.680
But you can see that there's a formula there.&nbsp;
And then I'm going to click on that one. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:29.680 --> 02:22:34.320
I'm going to drag it down. And what is going to do&nbsp;
is is going to maintain the relative reference is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:34.319 --> 02:22:42.639
going to say, at each step, take this cell that is&nbsp;
one over to the left, and multiply it times two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:42.640 --> 02:22:48.720
if you want to see what all the formulas look&nbsp;
like you can do Ctrl tilde where the tilde on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:48.720 --> 02:22:54.480
the keyboard is above the tab. And that shows you&nbsp;
all the formulas and you see it goes to a three to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:54.479 --> 02:22:59.759
a four to a five, so on down to a 12 and&nbsp;
multiply times 12. That's a relative reference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:59.760 --> 02:23:05.600
because all it says is go one to the left.&nbsp;
It doesn't say go to column A and although&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:05.600 --> 02:23:10.161
it has an a written here, but it just is&nbsp;
interpreted as the next one to the left.&nbsp;&nbsp;

